PROMPT -Use the provided reference image as the exact first frame and as the sole
authoritative source for every garment visible in the scene.
Create one continuous ultra-photorealistic vertical 9:16 fashion product demonstration video.
The scene contains one or more garments arranged on a real surface, such as a bed, table,
sofa, rug, linen backdrop or another surface already visible in the reference.
Before beginning the action, visually identify every physically distinct garment in the image.
Select exactly one real garment from the reference to present.
The selected garment must be one complete physically distinct piece.
Do not combine parts of different garments.
Do not select a decorative prop, accessory, shoe, bag, belt, book, flower or background
object.
If the image contains only one garment, select that garment.
If the image contains several garments, select only one of them.
Prefer a garment that is clearly visible, sufficiently separated from the others and can be
lifted without disturbing the entire composition.
RANDOMIZED SELECTION LOGIC:
When several garments are available, do not always select the first, largest, nearest or most
central garment.
Choose among the real garments using varied selection behavior.
Across separate generations, vary the selected garment whenever the generation system
allows variation.
Avoid repeatedly selecting the same garment by default.
Do not use a fixed preference based only on color, position or size.
The selected piece may be:
- a top;
- blouse;
- cropped top;
- bodysuit;
- vest;
- dress;
- skirt;
- shorts;
- trousers;
- cardigan;
- shirt;
- or another real garment present in the reference.
ABSOLUTE PRODUCT FIDELITY LOCK:
The selected garment must remain identical to the reference.
Preserve exactly:
- the garment category;
- the exact color;
- the exact color placement;
- the exact print;
- the exact print position;
- the exact logo;
- the exact written elements;
- the exact neckline;
- the exact collar;
- the exact sleeve length;
- the exact straps;
- the exact buttons;
- the exact button count;
- the exact pockets;
- the exact seams;
- the exact embroidery;
- the exact bows;
- the exact zipper;
- the exact waistband;
- the exact hem;
- the exact garment proportions;
- the exact fabric texture;
- the exact visible construction;
- the exact folds that are not physically changed by handling.
Do not redesign, simplify, beautify or reinterpret the selected garment.
Do not convert the garment into a similar item.
Do not change its length, width, cut, neckline or proportions.
Do not alter the print while the garment approaches the camera.
Do not make the garment transform, regenerate or replace itself during the movement.
UNSELECTED GARMENTS LOCK:
Every garment not selected must remain exactly where it is throughout the entire shot.
Preserve their exact:
- position;
- orientation;
- folds;
- spacing;
- scale;
- colors;
- prints;
- contact shadows;
- relationship with the surface.
The hands must not drag, cover, lift, rotate or displace the unselected garments.
If the selected garment is partially overlapping another piece, lift it carefully and naturally
without pulling the other garment.
No other garment may move as a consequence of the action, except for an extremely subtle
physically plausible release where the selected garment was resting.
HANDS:
Only one pair of adult feminine hands may appear.
Both hands belong to the same woman.
The hands must remain visually consistent for the entire video.
Show:
- realistic adult female anatomy;
- exactly five fingers on each hand;
- natural finger spacing;
- realistic joints;
- subtle veins;
- fine skin lines;
- visible skin pores;
- realistic knuckles;
- clean cuticles;
- natural nail shapes;
- short-to-medium professionally manicured nails;
- discreet nude, pale pink or neutral nail polish.
No rings.
No bracelets.
No watch.
No artificial nail distortion.
No extra hands.
No third hand.
No duplicated fingers.
No fused fingers.
No detached wrists.
ACTION STRUCTURE:
The camera begins from the exact reference frame.
The scene pauses briefly so the complete arrangement is clearly visible.
The same pair of feminine hands enters naturally from the lower edge of the frame.
The hands move toward the chosen garment with clear intention.
One hand grips the left side of the garment and the other hand grips the right side.
The grip must occur at structurally safe areas such as:
- shoulder edges;
- side seams;
- waistband corners;
- upper corners;
- reinforced edges;
- sleeve openings;
- stable fabric areas.
Do not grip the garment by:
- a fragile strap alone;
- a button;
- a zipper pull;
- embroidery;
- decorative applique;
- loose bow;
- delicate lace;
- thin drawstring;
- print;
- hard ornament.
The hands first separate the selected garment gently from the surface.
Then they lift the garment upward toward the camera in one smooth, continuous movement.
The garment remains facing the camera as much as physically possible.
Keep the front design visible.
The hands maintain the garment open enough to reveal its real silhouette.
Do not stretch it excessively.
Do not pull it unnaturally flat.
Do not fold it in half.
Do not twist it.
Do not shake it.
As the garment rises, show realistic fabric physics:
- natural gravity;
- believable drape;
- gentle fold formation;
- realistic fabric weight;
- subtle movement at loose edges;
- physically correct seam tension;
- natural response to the hands;
- realistic shadow changes;
- accurate depth as the garment approaches the lens.
The selected garment becomes progressively larger in the frame because it is physically
approaching the camera.
This must be a real lifting movement, not a digital zoom.
The hands bring the garment close to the camera without touching the lens.
Stop at a comfortable viewing distance where the fabric, stitching, neckline, print or primary
design details are clearly visible.
Hold the garment steady near the camera for the final portion of the video.
The final frame must show a close, clear and faithful view of the selected garment.
ONE-WAY ACTION — NO LOOP:
The action occurs exactly once.
The hands enter once.
They select one garment once.
They lift it once.
They approach the camera once.
They hold it near the camera once.
They do not lower the garment back to the surface.
They do not repeat the movement.
They do not return to the initial frame.
They do not place the garment back down.
The final frame must be visually different from the first frame.
The video ends while the garment is still held near the camera.
No seamless loop.
No cyclic movement.
No reverse motion.
No repeated pickup.
No repeated camera approach.
CAMERA:
The camera remains fixed above the composition.
Use an overhead or near-overhead angle between approximately 80 and 90 degrees.
The camera itself does not zoom.
The camera does not orbit.
The camera does not move backward.
The perceived enlargement comes only from the garment physically approaching the lens.
Maintain stable high-quality smartphone or commercial-camera behavior.
Minimal lens distortion.
No fisheye.
No handheld shake.
No sudden focus loss.
Use a natural focus transition from the surface to the garment as it approaches the camera.
Keep the main garment details sharp in the final close-up.
Do not allow autofocus pulsing.
Do not blur the garment completely.
LIGHTING:
Preserve the exact lighting established in the first frame.
Use stable soft natural daylight.
Maintain accurate product colors.
No flicker.
No moving light source.
No changing white balance.
No orange shift.
No blue shift.
No artificial glow.
No exposure pumping as the garment approaches the camera.
Allow only natural physically correct shadow changes caused by the garment being lifted.
REALISM:
The movement must look like a real woman physically choosing one garment from a styled
fashion composition and bringing it close to the camera for inspection.
Show:
- realistic hand pressure;
- natural fingertip contact;
- believable garment weight;
- realistic fabric response;
- accurate textile fibers;
- natural folds;
- physically plausible movement;
- subtle room ambience;
- authentic smartphone product-video realism.
Do not create a floating garment.
Do not make the clothing move before being touched.
Do not animate the print.
Do not make the garment rigid like cardboard.
Do not make the fabric liquid, rubbery or weightless.
STRICT NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS:
No looping.
No return to the first frame.
No repeated action.
No lowering the garment after lifting.
No second garment being selected.
No garment switching during the lift.
No garment transforming into another piece.
No product replacement.
No duplicated garment.
No missing garment.
No altered colors.
No changed print.
No changing logo.
No mirrored writing.
No changing button count.
No changing neckline.
No changing sleeve length.
No changing straps.
No changing waistband.
No changing hem.
No fabric melting.
No floating clothing.
No excessive stretching.
No shaking.
No aggressive pulling.
No extra hands.
No extra fingers.
No fused fingers.
No deformed hands.
No person entering the frame.
No face.
No body.
No mannequin.
No text overlays.
No captions.
No watermark.
Background sounds: soft natural room ambience and realistic fabric movement.
Music: subtle elegant fashion instrumental without vocals, low volume.
